# Three.js — Single Canvas

When working on Three.js, WebGL, or any 3D / shader effect in this project: **one canvas, one Three.js environment**.

## Hard rules

- Prefer a **single** `<canvas>` + one `WebGLRenderer` + one `Scene` + one animation loop.
- Add new effects as **factories / modules** that attach meshes/materials to that shared scene (see `src/components/threejs/`).
- Do **not** mount a second full Three.js canvas for StarlightRibbon, SunGlow, or other page effects.
- Do **not** run multiple `requestAnimationFrame` render loops that each call `renderer.render` for the same view.

## Why

Each extra canvas / renderer / loop costs CPU and GPU. Shared effects on one canvas stay lighter and stay in sync (time, scroll, resize).

## Pattern

```tsx
// ✅ Host owns the canvas once
// ThreeJSCanvas → scene + renderer + loop
//   createStarlightRibbon(scene)
//   createSunGlow(scene)

// ❌ Bad — separate canvases / environments per effect
// <StarlightRibbonCanvas /> + <SunGlowCanvas />
```

## FilmGrain (opt-in) — per-component intensity

- FilmGrain is **not** a fullscreen plane and **not** on every effect by default.
- Apply grain only to **selected** effects (same pattern as StarlightRibbon / GradientCloud): shared `filmGrainGlsl` / `filmGrainUniform` in that effect’s shader.
- Keep the “Effects with FilmGrain” table in `Doc/threeJS/ThreeJSComponents.md` updated.
- Do not add grain to SunGlow (or any other effect) unless the user asks for that effect specifically.

### Grain intensity is never global

When the user says change **`grain_intensity`** (or “more/less grain”), scope it **only** to the component that owns that grain control — never sync one slider into another effect.

| Component | Panel / live key | Defaults |
|---|---|---|
| **StarlightRibbon** (FilmGrain section) | `grain_intensity` | `FILMGRAIN_DEFAULTS.grain_intensity` |
| **GradientCloud** | `gradient_grain_intensity` → `gradientCloudLive.grain_intensity` | `GRADIENTCLOUD_DEFAULTS.grain_intensity` |

- Do **not** wire GradientCloud’s grain into Starlight (or the reverse).
- If a new effect gets FilmGrain later, give it its **own** intensity key + control under that effect’s section.

## Exceptions

- **GradientCloud** is allowed its **own canvas** so it can fill a div background (e.g. `CTADiv`). Do not put GradientCloud on the shared `ThreeJSCanvas`.
- Only add another canvas if the user explicitly asks for a separate WebGL context. Default for page-edge effects (StarlightRibbon, SunGlow, FilmGrain) remains one shared canvas.
